#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "9c9b5972-f775-5cbe-8e66-2a0309f24f0e" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-142" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2)$. Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} + x^{-1}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot x - x^{-2} \\\\\n&= 2x - x^{-2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= 2(2) - (2)^{-2} \\\\\n&= 4 - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063897 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13570-d504-53e6-8e02-0f1e85047a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve With Fractional Powers at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "3138f267-3903-510c-86e8-5597d3689262" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-143" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)$?" :question/worked-solution "This time, we'll use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nnotation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} + 3\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} + 3x^{2/3}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot x^{3} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot x^{-1/3} \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = 1} &= 4(1)^{3} + 2(1)^{-1/3} \\\\\n&= 4 + 2 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00a74f63-a4eb-5894-b4a4-eb4fd297a3f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Curve With a Certain Slope"}] :question/id #uuid "fe999c48-1afd-5949-b4f9-533c09511d95" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5295" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2+1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x)$. Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(3x^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&= 3\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(1) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2x + 0 \\\\\n&= 6x + 0 \\\\\n&= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= -1 \\\\\n6x &= -1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac 1 6\n$$"}]}
